Talk to the storage controller: probe it, then read a sector.

The root filesystem is an 18 MiB RAM disk with 44 files in it, and that is a
ceiling rather than a choice: the shim carries it inside the Android BOOT
partition, which is 32 MiB, and the kernel takes fourteen of those. The real
iOS 12 userland does not fit and never will -- nearly every binary it runs is
resolved out of dyld_shared_cache_arm64, 1.19 GiB on its own. Until the
kernel can read a storage device there is nothing for launchd to start, so
this is the next thing.

The shape it is headed for is a BSD block device, not an IOKit one. An
IOBlockStorageDevice under IOBlockStorageDriver is how a real handset gets
its IOMedia, but IOStorageFamily is a separate kext and is not in this tree,
so that route begins by building another kext into the kernel. It is not
needed. The root device is a dev_t, and bsd/dev/memdev.c -- already patched
here so that rd=md0 works -- is a plain bdevsw with a strategy routine. A
storage driver in that shape needs no IOKit at all; strategy() does real
transfers where memdev does a memcpy.

This patch is the two steps before that, in the order that makes each one
answerable on its own.

    dwmmc_probe=<mask>              read the identity registers (default: 1)
    dwmmc_read=<n> dwmmc_lba=<m>    send CMD17 n times from sector m (default: 0)

First, the identity registers. Bringing up a card means a clock setup, a
reset, CMD0/CMD1/CMD2/CMD3/CMD7, FIFO handling and timeouts, and when the
result of all that is silence there is no way to tell which step was wrong --
or whether the register block was ever mapped. VERID and HCON are read-only.
A plausible value out of them proves the mapping, the bus and the clock
together, in one line, before there is a single card command to be wrong
about.

The addresses are Samsung's own. The stock boot image carries four device
trees, one per board revision, and all four agree:

    dwmmc0@13540000   SPI 245   non-removable, 8-bit, HS400, use-smu
    dwmmc2@13560000   SPI 247   microSD slot, 4-bit, bypass-smu

so this handset's revision does not have to be identified to use them.

Only dwmmc0 is probed by default, and the reason is not caution for its own
sake. S-Boot reads the boot image off the internal eMMC, so dwmmc0 is powered
and clocked by the time this kernel runs. Nothing has brought up the SD
slot's clocks -- Android would have, from the CMU, and Android is not here.
Reading an ungated SFR block on Exynos does not return zero, it hangs the
bus. So dwmmc2 sits behind dwmmc_probe=2, and a boot that stops dead after
naming it has answered the question it was asked.

Second, the read -- and the probe is what made it this small. What came back
was not an idle controller but a working one:

    CLKENA 0x00000001   the card clock is running
    CLKDIV 0x00000001   divided by two, not the 400 kHz of an init
    CTYPE  0x00010000   bit 16: card 0 is on an 8-bit bus
    BLKSIZ 0x00000200   512-byte blocks, already set
    STATUS 0x00000906   bit 8: the card is driving DAT3; FIFO empty, not busy
    CTRL   0x00000010   interrupts on, DMA and IDMAC off -- S-Boot used PIO
    HCON   0x0be42541   bit 23: the hold register is implemented
    VERID  0x5342270a   revision 0x270a, so the FIFO is at 0x200, not 0x100

S-Boot left all of it behind. The card has already been through CMD0, CMD1,
CMD2, CMD3 and CMD7; it is selected and in the transfer state. So this sends
CMD17 and nothing else. READ_SINGLE_BLOCK goes to whichever card is selected,
needs no RCA, and needs to know nothing about what S-Boot did. If the card is
not in fact selected the controller answers with a response timeout, which
gets printed and named; that is a cheap way to be wrong.

Two values from the probe are used rather than assumed: HCON's hold-register
bit decides whether commands carry USE_HOLD_REG, and VERID's revision is what
puts the FIFO at 0x200.

The FIFO is drained while the transfer is running, not after it. It is 64
words deep and a sector is 128, so waiting for the data-transfer-over
interrupt first would stall the controller half way with a full FIFO and look
exactly like a dead card.

That is not a hypothetical. The first version of this read got as far as

    dwmmc:   CMD17 accepted, RESP0 0x00000900
    dwmmc:   data error: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO

which is a complete account of what went wrong. RESP0 0x900 is the card's R1
status: CURRENT_STATE 4, tran, and READY_FOR_DATA set -- so the card was
selected and the command path worked end to end. RINTSTS 0x424 is command
done, receive-FIFO data ready, and HTO, data starvation by the host. The data
arrived and nobody came for it, because that printf sat between the command
and the drain, and writing one line to this console means rendering glyphs
into a 720x1280 framebuffer: milliseconds, against a FIFO the card fills in
microseconds.

So command completion and the drain are now a single loop with no printf and
no delay in it -- not even a wait for the controller to accept the command in
front of it -- and everything worth saying is saved and said afterwards. The
data timeout in TMOUT is also set generously before the command rather than
inheriting whatever S-Boot tuned for its own loader, since that register is
what decides when HTO fires.

That fixed the transfer and exposed a second mistake, this one in judgement
rather than in code. The next attempt said:

    dwmmc:   data error: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO
    dwmmc:   128 of 128 words had arrived

All the data arrived. HTO was still latched, and this treated it as fatal, so
the sector was thrown away unread. But HTO is not a data error: it means the
host was late to the FIFO. The bits that say the data is wrong are DCRC, SBE
and EBE; DRTO says the card never sent it and FRUN says the FIFO ran under.
None of those fired, and all 128 words were there. A transfer that completes
has completed, whatever the controller made of the host's timing along the
way.

So HTO is out of the fatal mask and printed as a warning instead. That got
one step further and stopped again:

    dwmmc:   all 128 words arrived but the transfer never closed
    dwmmc:   at the end: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO

A whole sector, no corruption bits, and no DTO. The missing DTO follows from
HTO rather than being a second fault: once HTO fires the DesignWare core
abandons the data phase, so it never says the transfer is over.

What is left unexplained is why HTO fires at all. Not the host's timing: it
was raised identically whether the drain sat behind a framebuffer printf or
ran in a tight spin, which is a hundredfold difference in latency for no
difference in outcome. Two candidates remain -- the TMOUT write not taking
effect, and the SMU that this controller's device tree says is in its data
path -- and TMOUT is now read back and printed so the first can be settled
from the console.

Meanwhile the read is treated as having succeeded, because by the thing it
was written to test it has: a complete sector with none of the bits that
mean it is wrong. The bytes settle it in a way no status register can.

The bytes settled it:

    dwmmc:   TMOUT reads back 0xffffff40 (wrote 0xffffff40)
    dwmmc:   LBA 1:
    dwmmc:     000  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20
    dwmmc:     010  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20
    ...
    dwmmc:   CMD17 done: ... STATUS 0x00808d09

45 46 49 20 is "EFI ". LBA 1 was asked for and the first word of the GPT
header came back, so the card is being read and the data path works. But the
same word came back 128 times, TMOUT had taken effect so the timeout was not
the problem, and STATUS reported FIFO_COUNT = (0x808d09 >> 17) & 0x1fff = 64
-- the FIFO completely full and never emptied.

HCON had been printing the answer since the very first probe. Bits 9:7 are
H_DATA_WIDTH: 0 is a 16-bit FIFO, 1 is 32 and 2 is 64. HCON here is
0x0be42541, so bits 9:7 are 0b010 and this core's FIFO is 64 bits wide. A
32-bit read of it returns the low half and does not consume the entry. Hence
one word forever, a FIFO that never drains, a controller that gives up on the
host -- and an HTO that was never about the host's speed at all, which is
why making the drain a hundred times faster changed nothing.

The device tree agrees in hindsight: fifo-depth 64, at 8 bytes an entry, is
512 bytes, exactly one sector.

So the FIFO is now read in the width HCON reports, 64 bits here and 32 where
a core says so, and the sector buffer is declared as uint64_t so that the
wider access lands aligned.

Nothing is written, ever. There is no code here that can modify the card,
which on the phone's own storage is the only responsible way to start. The
controller is not reset either: CTRL_RESET would discard the clock, the bus
width and the card selection that all of this depends on, so only the FIFO is
cleared.

The register offsets in the new file are the DesignWare layout, written out
rather than taken from anywhere. They are facts about the hardware, but the
readable descriptions of this controller are in Linux and U-Boot, which are
GPL, and this tree is APSL.

One thing found on the way, recorded here because it decides where the root
volume is going to live: dwmmc0's tree says use-smu and dwmmc2's says
bypass-smu. The SMU is Samsung's inline encryption unit and it sits in the
internal eMMC's data path. Between that, and not having to find a free
partition on the phone's own storage, and being able to rewrite the root
volume with dd instead of a flash cycle, the SD card is the better target --
once the controller has been proven on the one that is already running.

The probe is called from bsd_init() straight after bsd_autoconf(), which is
late enough for printf to reach the console. The call site is deliberately
not next to inittodr(): hfs-filesystem-builtin and memdev-ramdisk-root both
insert there, they both apply after this one, and three patches editing the
same six lines is a merge conflict waiting for the next person.
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bsd/dev/dwmmc.c
@@ -0,0 +1,624 @@
+/*
+ * dwmmc.c -- a first look at the DesignWare Mobile Storage Host Controller
+ * on Exynos 7570.
+ *
+ * Why this exists. The root filesystem is a RAM disk the shim carries inside
+ * the Android BOOT partition, and that partition is 32 MiB. Fourteen of those
+ * megabytes are the kernel, so the root volume has a hard ceiling of 18 MiB
+ * and currently holds 44 files. The real iOS 12 userland does not fit in that
+ * and never will: almost every binary is resolved out of
+ * dyld_shared_cache_arm64, which is 1.19 GiB on its own. Until the kernel can
+ * read a real storage device, there is nothing for launchd to start.
+ *
+ * Why a BSD block device rather than an IOKit one. The obvious shape would be
+ * an IOBlockStorageDevice under IOBlockStorageDriver, which is how a real
+ * device gets its IOMedia. But IOStorageFamily is a separate kext and is not
+ * in this tree, so that road starts with building another kext into the
+ * kernel. It is not needed: the root device is a dev_t, and bsd/dev/memdev.c
+ * -- already patched here to make rd=md0 work -- is a plain bdevsw with a
+ * strategy routine. A storage driver in that shape needs no IOKit at all;
+ * strategy() does real transfers instead of a memcpy. That is where this is
+ * going.
+ *
+ * What this file does today: nothing but read the controller's identity
+ * registers and print them. That is deliberate. Talking to a card means a
+ * clock setup, a reset, the CMD0/CMD1/CMD2/CMD3/CMD7 sequence, FIFO handling
+ * and timeouts, and if the result is silence there is no way to tell which of
+ * those was wrong -- or whether the register block was even mapped. VERID and
+ * HCON answer that one question by themselves: they are read-only identity
+ * registers, so a plausible value proves the mapping, the bus and the
+ * controller's clock in a single line of output, before a single card command
+ * exists to be wrong.
+ *
+ * Where the addresses come from. The stock Samsung boot image carries four
+ * device trees, one per board revision; all four agree, which is what makes
+ * it safe not to care which revision this handset is:
+ *
+ *     dwmmc0@13540000   SPI 245   non-removable, 8-bit, HS400, use-smu
+ *     dwmmc2@13560000   SPI 247   microSD slot, 4-bit, bypass-smu
+ *
+ * dwmmc0 is the internal eMMC and dwmmc2 is the card slot. The probe defaults
+ * to dwmmc0 alone, for a reason worth stating: S-Boot reads the boot image
+ * off the eMMC, so that controller is powered and clocked by the time this
+ * kernel runs. The SD slot may not be -- Android would have brought its
+ * clocks up from the CMU, and nothing has. Reading the registers of an
+ * ungated block on Exynos does not return zero; it hangs the bus. So dwmmc2
+ * is behind dwmmc_probe=2, and a boot that stops dead after asking for it has
+ * answered the question.
+ *
+ * The register offsets below are the DesignWare layout. They are facts about
+ * the hardware and are written out here rather than copied from anywhere:
+ * the readable descriptions of this controller are in Linux and U-Boot, both
+ * GPL, and this file is APSL.
+ *
+ * The SMU in dwmmc0's device tree is Samsung's inline encryption unit. It
+ * sits in the data path of the internal eMMC and will have to be dealt with
+ * before that controller can be read from; the card slot does not have it,
+ * which is one of several reasons the root volume is headed for an SD card
+ * and not a partition of the phone's own storage.
+ *
+ *
+ * Reading a sector, and why there is no card bring-up here
+ *
+ * The probe answered more than it was asked. What came back was not an idle
+ * controller but a working one:
+ *
+ *     CLKENA 0x00000001   the card clock is running
+ *     CLKDIV 0x00000001   divided by two, not the 400 kHz of an init
+ *     CTYPE  0x00010000   bit 16: card 0 is on an 8-bit bus
+ *     BLKSIZ 0x00000200   512-byte blocks, already set
+ *     STATUS 0x00000906   bit 8: the card is driving DAT3; FIFO empty, not busy
+ *     CTRL   0x00000010   interrupts on, DMA and IDMAC off -- S-Boot used PIO
+ *
+ * S-Boot reads the boot image off this eMMC, and it left every bit of that
+ * behind. The card has already been through CMD0, CMD1, CMD2, CMD3 and CMD7;
+ * it is selected and in the transfer state. None of that has to be done
+ * again, and doing it anyway would mean writing the part of a storage driver
+ * that is hardest to debug blind -- clock programming and a state machine
+ * with no output -- in order to arrive back at the state already in the
+ * registers.
+ *
+ * So this sends CMD17 and nothing else. READ_SINGLE_BLOCK is addressed to
+ * whichever card is selected, so it needs no RCA and no knowledge of what
+ * S-Boot did. If the card is not in fact selected the controller answers with
+ * a response timeout, which this prints; that is a cheap way to be wrong.
+ *
+ * Two details from the probe are used rather than assumed. HCON bit 23 is
+ * set, so the hold register exists and commands must carry USE_HOLD_REG.
+ * VERID's revision is 0x270A, which is past 0x240A, so the FIFO is at 0x200
+ * rather than the 0x100 of the older IP.
+ *
+ * The FIFO is drained by polling, not by interrupt. There is no interrupt
+ * handler here and there should not be one yet: a polled read is the version
+ * whose failure modes are all visible in RINTSTS afterwards.
+ *
+ * Nothing is written, ever. This file has no code that can modify the card,
+ * which on the phone's own eMMC is the only responsible way to start.
+ */
+
+#include <sys/param.h>
+#include <sys/systm.h>
+#include <mach/vm_types.h>
+#include <libkern/libkern.h>
+#include <pexpert/pexpert.h>
+
+/*
+ * Declared here rather than included. No file under bsd/ includes
+ * <arm/machine_routines.h>, and this should not be the first: bsd/kern/
+ * kern_exit.c declares delay() the same way, which is the house pattern for
+ * reaching an osfmk routine from BSD.
+ */
+extern vm_offset_t ml_io_map(vm_offset_t phys_addr, vm_size_t size);
+extern void delay(int usec);
+
+/* Register offsets, from the controller. */
+#define DWMMC_CTRL              0x000
+#define DWMMC_PWREN             0x004
+#define DWMMC_CLKDIV            0x008
+#define DWMMC_CLKSRC            0x00c
+#define DWMMC_CLKENA            0x010
+#define DWMMC_TMOUT             0x014
+#define DWMMC_CTYPE             0x018
+#define DWMMC_BLKSIZ            0x01c
+#define DWMMC_BYTCNT            0x020
+#define DWMMC_INTMASK           0x024
+#define DWMMC_CMDARG            0x028
+#define DWMMC_CMD               0x02c
+#define DWMMC_RESP0             0x030
+#define DWMMC_RESP1             0x034
+#define DWMMC_RESP2             0x038
+#define DWMMC_RESP3             0x03c
+#define DWMMC_MINTSTS           0x040
+#define DWMMC_RINTSTS           0x044
+#define DWMMC_STATUS            0x048
+#define DWMMC_FIFOTH            0x04c
+#define DWMMC_CDETECT           0x050
+#define DWMMC_WRTPRT            0x054
+#define DWMMC_USRID             0x068
+#define DWMMC_VERID             0x06c
+#define DWMMC_HCON              0x070
+#define DWMMC_UHS_REG           0x074
+#define DWMMC_BMOD              0x080
+#define DWMMC_DATA              0x200
+
+/* One 4 KiB page covers the whole block; the device tree asks for 0x2000. */
+#define DWMMC_REGS_SIZE         0x2000
+
+/*
+ * The low half of VERID is the IP revision, and every member of this family
+ * reads as 0x2xxA -- 0x240A and 0x270A are the ones commonly seen. It is not
+ * a value a dead or unmapped block produces, which is the whole point of
+ * looking at it.
+ */
+#define DWMMC_VERID_REV(v)      ((v) & 0xffff)
+
+/* CTRL */
+#define DWMMC_CTRL_RESET        (1u << 0)
+#define DWMMC_CTRL_FIFO_RESET   (1u << 1)
+#define DWMMC_CTRL_DMA_RESET    (1u << 2)
+
+/*
+ * HCON describes how this instance of the core was built.
+ *
+ * Bits 9:7 are H_DATA_WIDTH and they are the whole story of this driver's
+ * first real bug: 0 means the FIFO is 16 bits wide, 1 means 32 and 2 means
+ * 64. This controller reads 0x0be42541, so bits 9:7 are 0b010 and the FIFO
+ * is 64 bits wide. A 32-bit read of a 64-bit FIFO returns the low half and
+ * does not consume the entry, so the same word comes back forever, the FIFO
+ * never empties, and the controller eventually gives up on the host -- which
+ * is exactly what was seen: "EFI " repeated 128 times, STATUS reporting the
+ * FIFO still full at 64 entries, and HTO raised no matter how fast the drain
+ * loop ran.
+ *
+ * The device tree's fifo-depth of 64 agrees: 64 entries of 8 bytes is 512,
+ * one whole sector.
+ *
+ * Bit 22 is IMPLEMENT_HOLD_REG. This part sets both 22 and 23, so whichever
+ * of the two the documentation means, commands here carry USE_HOLD_REG
+ * either way and nothing about this board turns on getting it right.
+ */
+#define DWMMC_HCON_DATA_WIDTH(h)        (((h) >> 7) & 0x7)
+#define DWMMC_HCON_HOLD_REG             (1u << 22)
+
+/* CMD */
+#define DWMMC_CMD_INDEX(i)      ((i) & 0x3f)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_RESP_EXP      (1u << 6)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_RESP_LONG     (1u << 7)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_CHECK_CRC     (1u << 8)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_DATA_EXP      (1u << 9)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_WRITE         (1u << 10)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_PRV_DAT_WAIT  (1u << 13)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_USE_HOLD_REG  (1u << 29)
+#define DWMMC_CMD_START         (1u << 31)
+
+/* RINTSTS / INTMASK */
+#define DWMMC_INT_RE            (1u << 1)       /* response error */
+#define DWMMC_INT_CMD_DONE      (1u << 2)
+#define DWMMC_INT_DTO           (1u << 3)       /* data transfer over */
+#define DWMMC_INT_RCRC          (1u << 6)       /* response CRC */
+#define DWMMC_INT_DCRC          (1u << 7)       /* data CRC */
+#define DWMMC_INT_RTO           (1u << 8)       /* response timeout */
+#define DWMMC_INT_DRTO          (1u << 9)       /* data read timeout */
+#define DWMMC_INT_HTO           (1u << 10)
+#define DWMMC_INT_FRUN          (1u << 11)
+#define DWMMC_INT_HLE           (1u << 12)      /* hardware locked write */
+#define DWMMC_INT_SBE           (1u << 13)
+#define DWMMC_INT_EBE           (1u << 15)
+#define DWMMC_INT_ALL           0xffffffffu
+
+#define DWMMC_INT_CMD_ERR       (DWMMC_INT_RE | DWMMC_INT_RTO | \
+	                         DWMMC_INT_RCRC | DWMMC_INT_HLE)
+/*
+ * HTO is deliberately not in here.
+ *
+ * It means the host was late to the FIFO -- flow control, not corruption.
+ * The bits that say the data is wrong are DCRC, SBE and EBE; DRTO says the
+ * card never sent it and FRUN says the FIFO ran under. HTO on its own is
+ * survivable, and on this kernel it has been survived: the first working
+ * read raised HTO and still delivered all 128 words with no CRC error. A
+ * transfer that completes is a transfer that completes, whatever the
+ * controller thought of the host's timing on the way.
+ *
+ * It is still worth printing, because once this drives a filesystem rather
+ * than one sector at boot, a host that is habitually late is a real problem.
+ */
+#define DWMMC_INT_DATA_FATAL    (DWMMC_INT_DCRC | DWMMC_INT_DRTO | \
+	                         DWMMC_INT_FRUN | DWMMC_INT_SBE | \
+	                         DWMMC_INT_EBE)
+
+/* STATUS: the FIFO occupancy, in words. */
+#define DWMMC_STATUS_FIFO_COUNT(s)      (((s) >> 17) & 0x1fff)
+
+/* MMC command indices used here. */
+#define MMC_READ_SINGLE_BLOCK   17
+
+#define DWMMC_SECTOR            512
+
+/*
+ * How long to wait for each step, in microseconds. Generous: a read that is
+ * going to work finishes in well under a millisecond at this clock, and the
+ * only cost of a long limit is how long a dead controller takes to say so.
+ */
+#define DWMMC_TIMEOUT_US        500000
+
+/*
+ * The data phase spins instead of sleeping: a sector at this clock is over
+ * in well under a millisecond, and a delay(1) between polls is long enough
+ * for the FIFO to fill and the controller to give up on us. This is the
+ * iteration budget for that spin -- large enough to be no limit in practice,
+ * small enough that a dead transfer still ends.
+ */
+#define DWMMC_SPINS             2000000
+
+struct dwmmc_unit {
+	const char      *name;
+	vm_offset_t     base;           /* physical */
+	uint32_t        bit;            /* in the dwmmc_probe boot argument */
+};
+
+static const struct dwmmc_unit dwmmc_units[] = {
+	{ "dwmmc0 (internal eMMC)", 0x13540000, 1 },
+	{ "dwmmc2 (microSD slot)",  0x13560000, 2 },
+};
+
+/* dwmmc_read=<sectors> and dwmmc_lba=<first>; see dwmmc_probe(). */
+static uint32_t dwmmc_read_count = 0;
+static uint32_t dwmmc_read_lba = 0;
+
+#define DWMMC_READ_MAX          4
+
+static uint32_t
+dwmmc_read(vm_offset_t regs, uint32_t off)
+{
+	return *(volatile uint32_t *)(regs + off);
+}
+
+static void
+dwmmc_write(vm_offset_t regs, uint32_t off, uint32_t val)
+{
+	*(volatile uint32_t *)(regs + off) = val;
+}
+
+/* Name the bits of RINTSTS that mean something went wrong. */
+static void
+dwmmc_print_errors(const char *what, uint32_t sts)
+{
+	printf("dwmmc:   %s: RINTSTS 0x%08x%s%s%s%s%s%s%s%s%s\n", what, sts,
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_RE)   ? " RE" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_RTO)  ? " RTO(response timeout)" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_RCRC) ? " RCRC" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_HLE)  ? " HLE" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_DCRC) ? " DCRC" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_DRTO) ? " DRTO(data timeout)" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_HTO)  ? " HTO" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_SBE)  ? " SBE" : "",
+	    (sts & DWMMC_INT_EBE)  ? " EBE" : "");
+}
+
+/*
+ * One 512-byte sector, by CMD17, using whatever state the bootloader left.
+ *
+ * buf is 128 words. The FIFO is drained as it fills rather than after the
+ * transfer: it is 64 words deep and a sector is 128, so waiting for DTO
+ * first would stall the controller half way with a full FIFO and look
+ * exactly like a dead card.
+ */
+static int
+dwmmc_read_block(vm_offset_t regs, uint32_t hcon, uint32_t lba, void *buf)
+{
+	/*
+	 * The FIFO is read in the width the core was built with, which is
+	 * what HCON says and not what is convenient. See DWMMC_HCON_DATA_WIDTH.
+	 */
+	const uint32_t fifo_bytes = 2u << DWMMC_HCON_DATA_WIDTH(hcon);
+	uint8_t *out = buf;
+	uint32_t cmd, sts, resp;
+	uint32_t got = 0;               /* bytes */
+	int us;
+
+	if (fifo_bytes != 4 && fifo_bytes != 8) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   HCON says the FIFO is %u bytes wide, which "
+		    "this does not know how to read\n", fifo_bytes);
+		return -1;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * A clean FIFO and a clean status, and nothing else. CTRL_RESET would
+	 * discard the clock, bus width and card selection that this depends
+	 * on, so the controller itself is not reset.
+	 */
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_CTRL,
+	    dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CTRL) | DWMMC_CTRL_FIFO_RESET);
+	for (us = 0; us < DWMMC_TIMEOUT_US; us++) {
+		if (!(dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CTRL) & DWMMC_CTRL_FIFO_RESET)) {
+			break;
+		}
+		delay(1);
+	}
+	if (us == DWMMC_TIMEOUT_US) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   FIFO reset never completed\n");
+		return -1;
+	}
+
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_RINTSTS, DWMMC_INT_ALL);
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_BLKSIZ, DWMMC_SECTOR);
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_BYTCNT, DWMMC_SECTOR);
+
+	/*
+	 * A generous data timeout. The upper 24 bits are counted in card
+	 * clock cycles and bound how long the controller will wait for the
+	 * host to service the FIFO before raising HTO.
+	 */
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_TMOUT, 0xffffff40u);
+
+	/*
+	 * Block addressing, not byte. Every eMMC larger than 2 GiB uses it,
+	 * and this one is 16.
+	 */
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_CMDARG, lba);
+
+	cmd = DWMMC_CMD_INDEX(MMC_READ_SINGLE_BLOCK)
+	    | DWMMC_CMD_RESP_EXP | DWMMC_CMD_CHECK_CRC
+	    | DWMMC_CMD_DATA_EXP | DWMMC_CMD_PRV_DAT_WAIT
+	    | DWMMC_CMD_START;
+	if (hcon & DWMMC_HCON_HOLD_REG) {
+		cmd |= DWMMC_CMD_USE_HOLD_REG;
+	}
+
+	dwmmc_write(regs, DWMMC_CMD, cmd);
+
+	/*
+	 * From here to the end of the transfer there is no printf and no
+	 * delay, and that is deliberate.
+	 *
+	 * An earlier version printed the card's response between the command
+	 * and the drain. Writing one line to this console means rendering
+	 * glyphs into a 720x1280 framebuffer, which takes milliseconds; the
+	 * card fills the FIFO in microseconds. So command completion and the
+	 * drain are one loop, with not even a wait for the controller to
+	 * accept the command in front of it, and anything worth saying is
+	 * saved and said afterwards.
+	 */
+	for (us = 0; us < DWMMC_SPINS; us++) {
+		uint32_t now = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_RINTSTS);
+		uint32_t avail;
+
+		if (now & DWMMC_INT_CMD_ERR) {
+			sts = now;
+			goto cmd_failed;
+		}
+
+		/* FIFO_COUNT is in entries, and an entry is fifo_bytes wide. */
+		avail = DWMMC_STATUS_FIFO_COUNT(dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_STATUS));
+		while (avail-- > 0 && got < DWMMC_SECTOR) {
+			if (fifo_bytes == 8) {
+				*(uint64_t *)(void *)(out + got) =
+				    *(volatile uint64_t *)(regs + DWMMC_DATA);
+			} else {
+				*(uint32_t *)(void *)(out + got) =
+				    *(volatile uint32_t *)(regs + DWMMC_DATA);
+			}
+			got += fifo_bytes;
+		}
+
+		if (now & DWMMC_INT_DATA_FATAL) {
+			sts = now;
+			goto data_failed;
+		}
+		if (got == DWMMC_SECTOR && (now & DWMMC_INT_DTO)) {
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	sts = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_RINTSTS);
+	resp = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_RESP0);
+
+	if (got < DWMMC_SECTOR) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   short read: %u of %u bytes, RESP0 0x%08x\n",
+		    got, (uint32_t)DWMMC_SECTOR, resp);
+		dwmmc_print_errors("at the end", sts);
+		return -1;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * A whole sector and none of the bits that mean it is wrong. HTO and
+	 * a missing DTO are reported but not treated as failure: HTO means
+	 * the host was late to the FIFO, and once it fires the core abandons
+	 * the data phase, so the absent DTO follows from it. With the FIFO
+	 * read at its real width neither should appear again, and if they do
+	 * the bytes below are still the thing that settles it.
+	 */
+	if (sts & (DWMMC_INT_HTO | DWMMC_INT_DTO)) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   %s, %s\n",
+		    (sts & DWMMC_INT_HTO) ? "HTO raised" : "no HTO",
+		    (sts & DWMMC_INT_DTO) ? "transfer closed" : "no DTO");
+	}
+
+	printf("dwmmc:   CMD17 done: RESP0 0x%08x (state %u%s), "
+	    "RINTSTS 0x%08x, STATUS 0x%08x, FIFO %u bytes wide\n",
+	    resp, (resp >> 9) & 0xf,
+	    ((resp >> 9) & 0xf) == 4 ? " = tran" : "", sts,
+	    dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_STATUS), fifo_bytes);
+	return 0;
+
+cmd_failed:
+	resp = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_RESP0);
+	dwmmc_print_errors("command rejected", sts);
+	if (sts & DWMMC_INT_RTO) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   a response timeout here means the card is "
+		    "not selected; it needs a full CMD0..CMD7 bring-up after "
+		    "all\n");
+	}
+	return -1;
+
+data_failed:
+	dwmmc_print_errors("data error", sts);
+	printf("dwmmc:   %u of %u bytes had arrived\n", got,
+	    (uint32_t)DWMMC_SECTOR);
+	return -1;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The first 64 bytes, and whatever the sector announces itself to be.
+ *
+ * On a Samsung handset LBA 0 is a protective MBR, so the 0x55AA at offset
+ * 510 is the thing to look for: finding it means the bytes came off the card
+ * and arrived in the right order, which no amount of plausible-looking hex
+ * can establish on its own.
+ */
+static void
+dwmmc_dump_sector(uint32_t lba, const void *buf)
+{
+	const unsigned char *b = (const unsigned char *)buf;
+	int row;
+
+	printf("dwmmc:   LBA %u:\n", lba);
+	for (row = 0; row < 4; row++) {
+		const unsigned char *p = b + row * 16;
+
+		printf("dwmmc:     %03x  %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x "
+		    "%02x  %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x %02x\n",
+		    row * 16,
+		    p[0], p[1], p[2], p[3], p[4], p[5], p[6], p[7],
+		    p[8], p[9], p[10], p[11], p[12], p[13], p[14], p[15]);
+	}
+
+	if (b[510] == 0x55 && b[511] == 0xaa) {
+		printf("dwmmc:     ends 55 aa -- a partition table signature\n");
+	} else if (b[0] == 'E' && b[1] == 'F' && b[2] == 'I' && b[3] == ' '
+	    && b[4] == 'P' && b[5] == 'A' && b[6] == 'R' && b[7] == 'T') {
+		printf("dwmmc:     starts \"EFI PART\" -- a GPT header\n");
+	} else {
+		printf("dwmmc:     no signature here (bytes 510-511 are "
+		    "%02x %02x)\n", b[510], b[511]);
+	}
+}
+
+static void
+dwmmc_probe_one(const struct dwmmc_unit *unit)
+{
+	vm_offset_t regs;
+	uint32_t verid, hcon, usrid, ctrl, status, cdetect;
+	uint32_t clkena, clkdiv, ctype, blksiz, fifoth, bmod;
+
+	regs = ml_io_map(unit->base, DWMMC_REGS_SIZE);
+	if (regs == 0) {
+		printf("dwmmc: %s: ml_io_map(0x%lx) failed\n",
+		    unit->name, (unsigned long)unit->base);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	printf("dwmmc: %s at 0x%lx, mapped\n",
+	    unit->name, (unsigned long)unit->base);
+
+	/*
+	 * VERID first and on its own line. If the block is not there, this is
+	 * where the boot stops, and the line above will be the last thing on
+	 * the screen -- which says exactly as much as a value would have.
+	 */
+	verid = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_VERID);
+	hcon = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_HCON);
+	usrid = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_USRID);
+
+	printf("dwmmc:   VERID 0x%08x (rev 0x%04x)  HCON 0x%08x  USRID 0x%08x\n",
+	    verid, DWMMC_VERID_REV(verid), hcon, usrid);
+
+	ctrl = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CTRL);
+	status = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_STATUS);
+	cdetect = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CDETECT);
+	bmod = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_BMOD);
+
+	printf("dwmmc:   CTRL 0x%08x  STATUS 0x%08x  CDETECT 0x%08x  "
+	    "BMOD 0x%08x\n", ctrl, status, cdetect, bmod);
+
+	clkena = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CLKENA);
+	clkdiv = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CLKDIV);
+	ctype = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_CTYPE);
+	blksiz = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_BLKSIZ);
+	fifoth = dwmmc_read(regs, DWMMC_FIFOTH);
+
+	printf("dwmmc:   CLKENA 0x%08x  CLKDIV 0x%08x  CTYPE 0x%08x  "
+	    "BLKSIZ 0x%08x  FIFOTH 0x%08x\n",
+	    clkena, clkdiv, ctype, blksiz, fifoth);
+
+	/*
+	 * The verdict, so that a photograph of the screen does not have to be
+	 * read against a datasheet.
+	 *
+	 * CDETECT is reported but deliberately not judged: bit 0 is low when a
+	 * card is present, and dwmmc0's tree says broken-card-detect, so on
+	 * the eMMC the bit means nothing at all.
+	 */
+	if (verid == 0 || verid == 0xffffffff) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   VERID reads as 0x%08x -- the block is not "
+		    "responding\n", verid);
+	} else if ((DWMMC_VERID_REV(verid) & 0xf000) != 0x2000) {
+		printf("dwmmc:   revision 0x%04x is not one this family "
+		    "produces; check the address\n", DWMMC_VERID_REV(verid));
+	} else {
+		printf("dwmmc:   alive: mapping, bus and clock all work\n");
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Reading is asked for rather than assumed. The probe is safe on any
+	 * controller; a command is not, and this one is the phone's own
+	 * storage.
+	 */
+	if (dwmmc_read_count > 0) {
+		/* uint64_t, so a 64-bit FIFO read lands aligned. */
+		uint64_t buf[DWMMC_SECTOR / 8];
+		uint32_t n;
+
+		for (n = 0; n < dwmmc_read_count; n++) {
+			if (dwmmc_read_block(regs, hcon,
+			    dwmmc_read_lba + n, buf) != 0) {
+				printf("dwmmc:   read of LBA %u failed\n",
+				    dwmmc_read_lba + n);
+				break;
+			}
+			dwmmc_dump_sector(dwmmc_read_lba + n, buf);
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Called from bsd_init() after bsd_autoconf(), which is late enough for
+ * printf to reach the console and early enough to be before the root mount
+ * this is eventually meant to serve.
+ *
+ * dwmmc_probe=<mask>: bit 0 is dwmmc0, bit 1 is dwmmc2. Default 1. See the
+ * header on why dwmmc2 is not probed unless it is asked for.
+ *
+ * dwmmc_read=<sectors>, dwmmc_lba=<first>: send CMD17 that many times, from
+ * that sector, and print what comes back. Default 0, which sends no command
+ * at all -- the probe only reads identity registers, and that is safe on any
+ * controller, whereas a command is not.
+ */
+void dwmmc_probe(void);
+
+void
+dwmmc_probe(void)
+{
+	uint32_t mask = 1;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("dwmmc_probe", &mask, sizeof(mask));
+	(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("dwmmc_read", &dwmmc_read_count,
+	    sizeof(dwmmc_read_count));
+	(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("dwmmc_lba", &dwmmc_read_lba,
+	    sizeof(dwmmc_read_lba));
+
+	if (dwmmc_read_count > DWMMC_READ_MAX) {
+		dwmmc_read_count = DWMMC_READ_MAX;
+	}
+
+	if (mask == 0) {
+		return;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < sizeof(dwmmc_units) / sizeof(dwmmc_units[0]); i++) {
+		if (mask & dwmmc_units[i].bit) {
+			dwmmc_probe_one(&dwmmc_units[i]);
+		}
+	}
+}
